before:
This complete TypeScript example needs a TypeScript compiler or this lesson's TypeScript runner; ordinary JavaScript cannot read interface declarations.
```typescript
interface Runner {
  name: string;
  laps: number;
}
function describeRunner(runner: Runner): string {
  return `${runner.name} completed ${runner.laps} laps.`;
}
const runner: Runner = { name: 'Ivy', laps: 4 };
console.log(describeRunner(runner));
```
The result is Ivy completed 4 laps. An interface describes an object's fields: name must be text and laps must be a number. It does not create the object itself.

Add this second example after the first block:
```typescript
const another = { name: 'Sol', laps: 9 };
const sentence = describeRunner(another);
console.log(sentence);
console.log(typeof another.laps);
```
The outputs are Sol completed 9 laps. and number. TypeScript infers another's field types from their values: inference means it works out a type without an explicit annotation. The function uses the supplied object, so it works for different runners rather than hard-coding Ivy's name.

after:
An interface describes an object's required parts. This Runner needs a text name and a numeric lap count.
```typescript
interface Runner {
  name: string;
  laps: number;
}
function describeRunner(runner: Runner): string {
  return `${runner.name} completed ${runner.laps} laps.`;
}
console.log(describeRunner({ name: "Ivy", laps: 4 }));
```
The output is Ivy completed 4 laps.
